{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789825248492,"sessionID":"ses_f461fa331ffe0Vx8rAYcMiXg6i","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9e5ec28001DY5DHn0FI4BRh6","sessionID":"ses_f461fa331ffe0Vx8rAYcMiXg6i","messageID":"msg_0b9e5e346001lztbrTHo1JOMeg","type":"tool","id":"call_00_aU1QIcaCEmzFmD6YDL5u1430","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/tmp/opencode/work/filing_pages.txt","offset":1945,"limit":120},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/work/filing_pages.txt, lines 1945-2064\n1945: [[page 25]]\n1946: \n1947: Table of Contents\n1948: \n1949: with\n1950: respect to this proposal is advisory only and will not be binding on Mac-Gray. Therefore, regardless of whether stockholders vote to approve the \"golden parachute\" compensation, if the merger is\n1951: approved by the Mac-Gray stockholders and completed, the \"golden parachute\" compensation will still be paid to the Mac-Gray named executive officers to the extent payable in accordance with the terms\n1952: of such compensation arrangements. \n1953: \n1954:  Information\n1955: required by Item 402(t) of Regulation S-K promulgated under the Securities Act regarding the compensation of our named executive officers that is based on or\n1956: otherwise relates to the merger is included on page 56 of this proxy statement, under the heading \"The Merger—Interests of Mac-Gray's Directors and Executive Officers in the\n1957: Merger—Golden Parachute Compensation for Mac-Gray Named Executive Officers.\" \n1958: \n1959:  The Mac-Gray Board of Directors unanimously recommends that holders of Mac-Gray common stock vote \"FOR\" the proposal to approve, on an advisory (non-binding)\n1960: basis, the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that could become payable to the named executive officers of Mac-Gray in connection with the merger pursuant to the terms of the merger agreement\n1961: and pre-existing severance arrangements.\n1962: \n1963:  The Adjournment Proposal \n1964: \n1965: (Item 3\n1966: on the Proxy Card) \n1967: \n1968:  Mac-Gray\n1969: is asking its stockholders to approve a proposal for one or more adjournments or postponements of the special meeting, if necessary, to solicit additional proxies if Mac-Gray\n1970: has not obtained sufficient affirmative stockholder votes to adopt the merger agreement. If the Mac-Gray stockholders approve the adjournment proposal, Mac-Gray could adjourn or postpone the special\n1971: meeting, and any adjourned session of the special meeting, and use the additional time to solicit additional proxies. \n1972: \n1973:  If,\n1974: at the special meeting, the number of shares of Mac-Gray common stock present in person or by proxy and voting in favor of the proposal to adopt the merger agreement is not\n1975: sufficient to approve that proposal, Mac-Gray may move to adjourn the special meeting in order to enable the Mac-Gray Board of Directors to solicit additional proxies for the adoption of the merger\n1976: agreement. In that event, Mac-Gray will ask its stockholders to vote only upon the adjournment proposal, and not the merger agreement proposal. The approval of the adjournment proposal requires a\n1977: majority of the votes properly cast. If you abstain from voting, fail to cast your vote, in person or by proxy, or fail to give voting instructions to your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other\n1978: nominee, it will have no effect on the proposal to adjourn the special meeting. \n1979: \n1980:  The\n1981: adjournment proposal relates only to an adjournment of the special meeting occurring for purposes of soliciting additional proxies for approval of the merger agreement proposal in\n1982: the event that there are insufficient votes to approve that proposal. Mac-Gray retains full authority to the extent set forth in its bylaws and Delaware law (subject to the terms of the merger\n1983: agreement) to adjourn the special meeting for any other purpose, or to postpone the special meeting before it is convened, without the consent of any Mac-Gray stockholders. \n1984: \n1985:  The Mac-Gray Board of Directors unanimously recommends that holders of Mac-Gray common stock vote \"FOR\" the adjournment proposal, if\n1986: necessary.\n1987: \n1988: 25\n1989: \n1990:  ZEQ.=2,SEQ=31,EFW=\"2217482\",CP=\"MAC-GRAY CORPORATION\",DN=\"1\",CHK=934298,FOLIO='25',FILE='DISK112:[13ZCX1.13ZCX46801]DF46801A.;9',USER='HYUNG',CD=';2-DEC-2013;13:16' \n1991: \n1992: [[page 26]]\n1993: \n1994: Table of Contents\n1995: \n1996:  THE COMPANIES \n1997: \n1998:  Mac-Gray Corporation is the second largest laundry facilities management contractor in\n1999: the United States. Through our portfolio of card- and coin-operated laundry equipment located in laundry facilities across the country, we provide laundry convenience to residents of multi-unit\n2000: housing such as apartment buildings, condominiums, colleges and university residence halls, public housing complexes, and hotels and motels. Based on our ongoing survey of colleges and universities,\n2001: we believe that we are the largest provider of such services to the college and university market in the United States. \n2002: \n2003: Mac-Gray\n2004: Corporation\n2005: \n2006: 404 Wyman Street, Suite 400\n2007: \n2008: Waltham, Massachusetts 02451\n2009: \n2010: (781) 487-7600 \n2011: \n2012:  CSC ServiceWorks, Inc., with over one million machines in service, is the leading provider of multi-family housing and commercial\n2013: laundry solutions as well as the industry leader in air vending services at convenience stores and gas stations. CSC's family of businesses includes: Coinmach Corp., ASI Campus Laundry Solutions, SDI\n2014: Laundry Solutions, Sparkle Solutions Corp., Appliance Warehouse of America, AIR-serv, AirValet and Super Laundry. CSC has a workforce of over 2,250 dedicated professionals throughout the United\n2015: States, Canada and Europe. \n2016: \n2017:  CSC\n2018: ServiceWorks, Inc.\n2019: \n2020: 303 Sunnyside Boulevard #70\n2021: \n2022: Plainview, New York 11803\n2023: \n2024: (516) 349-8555 \n2025: \n2026:  CSC ServiceWorks Holdings, Inc. is a Delaware corporation and the sole stockholder of CSC. \n2027: \n2028:  CSC\n2029: ServiceWorks Holdings, Inc.\n2030: \n2031: 303 Sunnyside Boulevard #70\n2032: \n2033: Plainview, New York 11803\n2034: \n2035: (516) 349-8555 \n2036: \n2037:  Spin Holdco Inc. is a Delaware corporation and a wholly-owned subsidiary of CSC. \n2038: \n2039:  Spin\n2040: Holdco Inc.\n2041: \n2042: 303 Sunnyside Boulevard #70\n2043: \n2044: Plainview, New York 11803\n2045: \n2046: (516) 349-8555 \n2047: \n2048:  CSC Fenway, Inc. is a Delaware corporation and a wholly-owned subsidiary of Parent. \n2049: \n2050:  CSC\n2051: Fenway, Inc.\n2052: \n2053: 303 Sunnyside Boulevard #70\n2054: \n2055: Plainview, New York 11803\n2056: \n2057: (516) 349-8555 \n2058: \n2059: 26\n2060: \n2061:  ZEQ.=3,SEQ=32,EFW=\"2217482\",CP=\"MAC-GRAY CORPORATION\",DN=\"1\",CHK=53809,FOLIO='26',FILE='DISK112:[13ZCX1.13ZCX46801]DF46801A.;9',USER='HYUNG',CD=';2-DEC-2013;13:16' \n2062: \n2063: [[page 27]]\n2064: \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 2065]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/work/filing_pages.txt, lines 1945-2064\n1945: [[page 25]]\n1946: \n1947: Table of Contents\n1948: \n1949: with\n1950: respect to this proposal is advisory only and will not be binding on Mac-Gray. Therefore, regardless of whether stockholders vote to approve the \"golden parachute\" compensation, if the merger is\n1951: approved by the Mac-Gray stockholders and completed, the \"golden parachute\" compensation will still be paid to the Mac-Gray named executive officers to the extent payable in accordance with the terms\n1952: of such compensation arrangements. \n1953: \n1954:  Information\n1955: required by Item 402(t) of Regulation S-K promulgated under the Securities Act regarding the compensation of our named executive officers that is based on or\n1956: otherwise relates to the merger is included on page 56 of this proxy statement, under the heading \"The Merger—Interests of Mac-Gray's Directors and Executive Officers in the\n1957: Merger—Golden Parachute Compensation for Mac-Gray Named Executive Officers.\" \n1958: \n1959:  The Mac-Gray Board of Directors unanimously recommends that holders of Mac-Gray common stock vote \"FOR\" the proposal to approve, on an advisory (non-binding)\n1960: basis, the \"golden parachute\" compensation payable or that could become payable to the named executive officers of Mac-Gray in connection with the merger pursuant to the terms of the merger agreement\n1961: and pre-existing severance arrangements.\n1962: \n1963:  The Adjournment Proposal \n1964: \n1965: (Item 3\n1966: on the Proxy Card) \n1967: \n1968:  Mac-Gray\n1969: is asking its stockholders to approve a proposal for one or more adjournments or postponements of the special meeting, if necessary, to solicit additional proxies if Mac-Gray\n1970: has not obtained sufficient affirmative stockholder votes to adopt the merger agreement. If the Mac-Gray stockholders approve the adjournment proposal, Mac-Gray could adjourn or postpone the special\n1971: meeting, and any adjourned session of the special meeting, and use the additional time to solicit additional proxies. \n1972: \n1973:  If,\n1974: at the special meeting, the number of shares of Mac-Gray common stock present in person or by proxy and voting in favor of the proposal to adopt the merger agreement is not\n1975: sufficient to approve that proposal, Mac-Gray may move to adjourn the special meeting in order to enable the Mac-Gray Board of Directors to solicit additional proxies for the adoption of the merger\n1976: agreement. In that event, Mac-Gray will ask its stockholders to vote only upon the adjournment proposal, and not the merger agreement proposal. The approval of the adjournment proposal requires a\n1977: majority of the votes properly cast. If you abstain from voting, fail to cast your vote, in person or by proxy, or fail to give voting instructions to your brokerage firm, bank, trust or other\n1978: nominee, it will have no effect on the proposal to adjourn the special meeting. \n1979: \n1980:  The\n1981: adjournment proposal relates only to an adjournment of the special meeting occurring for purposes of soliciting additional proxies for approval of the merger agreement proposal in\n1982: the event that there are insufficient votes to approve that proposal. Mac-Gray retains full authority to the extent set forth in its bylaws and Delaware law (subject to the terms of the merger\n1983: agreement) to adjourn the special meeting for any other purpose, or to postpone the special meeting before it is convened, without the consent of any Mac-Gray stockholders. \n1984: \n1985:  The Mac-Gray Board of Directors unanimously recommends that holders of Mac-Gray common stock vote \"FOR\" the adjournment proposal, if\n1986: necessary.\n1987: \n1988: 25\n1989: \n1990:  ZEQ.=2,SEQ=31,EFW=\"2217482\",CP=\"MAC-GRAY CORPORATION\",DN=\"1\",CHK=934298,FOLIO='25',FILE='DISK112:[13ZCX1.13ZCX46801]DF46801A.;9',USER='HYUNG',CD=';2-DEC-2013;13:16' \n1991: \n1992: [[page 26]]\n1993: \n1994: Table of Contents\n1995: \n1996:  THE COMPANIES \n1997: \n1998:  Mac-Gray Corporation is the second largest laundry facilities management contractor in\n1999: the United States. Through our portfolio of card- and coin-operated laundry equipment located in laundry facilities across the country, we provide laundry convenience to residents of multi-unit\n2000: housing such as apartment buildings, condominiums, colleges and university residence halls, public housing complexes, and hotels and motels. Based on our ongoing survey of colleges and universities,\n2001: we believe that we are the largest provider of such services to the college and university market in the United States. \n2002: \n2003: Mac-Gray\n2004: Corporation\n2005: \n2006: 404 Wyman Street, Suite 400\n2007: \n2008: Waltham, Massachusetts 02451\n2009: \n2010: (781) 487-7600 \n2011: \n2012:  CSC ServiceWorks, Inc., with over one million machines in service, is the leading provider of multi-family housing and commercial\n2013: laundry solutions as well as the industry leader in air vending services at convenience stores and gas stations. CSC's family of businesses includes: Coinmach Corp., ASI Campus Laundry Solutions, SDI\n2014: Laundry Solutions, Sparkle Solutions Corp., Appliance Warehouse of America, AIR-serv, AirValet and Super Laundry. CSC has a workforce of over 2,250 dedicated professionals throughout the United\n2015: States, Canada and Europe. \n2016: \n2017:  CSC\n2018: ServiceWorks, Inc.\n2019: \n2020: 303 Sunnyside Boulevard #70\n2021: \n2022: Plainview, New York 11803\n2023: \n2024: (516) 349-8555 \n2025: \n2026:  CSC ServiceWorks Holdings, Inc. is a Delaware corporation and the sole stockholder of CSC. \n2027: \n2028:  CSC\n2029: ServiceWorks Holdings, Inc.\n2030: \n2031: 303 Sunnyside Boulevard #70\n2032: \n2033: Plainview, New York 11803\n2034: \n2035: (516) 349-8555 \n2036: \n2037:  Spin Holdco Inc. is a Delaware corporation and a wholly-owned subsidiary of CSC. \n2038: \n2039:  Spin\n2040: Holdco Inc.\n2041: \n2042: 303 Sunnyside Boulevard #70\n2043: \n2044: Plainview, New York 11803\n2045: \n2046: (516) 349-8555 \n2047: \n2048:  CSC Fenway, Inc. is a Delaware corporation and a wholly-owned subsidiary of Parent. \n2049: \n2050:  CSC\n2051: Fenway, Inc.\n2052: \n2053: 303 Sunnyside Boulevard #70\n2054: \n2055: Plainview, New York 11803\n2056: \n2057: (516) 349-8555 \n2058: \n2059: 26\n2060: \n2061:  ZEQ.=3,SEQ=32,EFW=\"2217482\",CP=\"MAC-GRAY CORPORATION\",DN=\"1\",CHK=53809,FOLIO='26',FILE='DISK112:[13ZCX1.13ZCX46801]DF46801A.;9',USER='HYUNG',CD=';2-DEC-2013;13:16' \n2062: \n2063: [[page 27]]\n2064: \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 2065]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\