{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789825502527,"sessionID":"ses_f461fa331ffe0Vx8rAYcMiXg6i","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9e9c86e001vIEwd074M6w47g","sessionID":"ses_f461fa331ffe0Vx8rAYcMiXg6i","messageID":"msg_0b9e9b003001LxbfkClXVhrL5N","type":"tool","id":"call_00_YwLAFRdoeylBPk7Dy1IU7764","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/opencode/work && python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nfrom openpyxl import load_workbook\nwb = load_workbook('/home/uctpiaj/work/extraction/mac-gray.xlsx')\nfor name in ['Rounds','Questions','Deal facts']:\n    ws = wb[name]\n    print(f\"===== {name} =====\")\n    for row in ws.iter_rows(values_only=True):\n        print(\" | \".join(\"\" if v is None else str(v) for v in row))\n    print()\n# check date cells are real dates and formats\nws = wb['Deal ledger']\nfrom datetime import date\nbad = []\nfor r in range(2, ws.max_row+1):\n    for c in (20,21,22):\n        v = ws.cell(row=r, column=c).value\n        if v is not None and not isinstance(v, date):\n            bad.append((r,c,v))\nprint(\"non-date values in date cols:\", bad)\nprint(\"number formats:\", ws.cell(row=2,column=20).number_format, ws.cell(row=2,column=8).number_format)\nprint(\"deal facts date cells:\", wb['Deal facts'].cell(row=6,column=2).value, wb['Deal facts'].cell(row=6,column=2).number_format)\nprint(\"header bold check:\", wb['Deal ledger'].cell(row=1,column=1).font.bold, wb['Rounds'].cell(row=1,column=1).font.bold)\nprint(\"wrap check:\", wb['Deal ledger'].cell(row=2,column=16).alignment.wrap_text)\nPYEOF"},"output":"===== Rounds =====\nProcess | Round | Opened | How opened | Who was in | Due dates | Deadline outcome | Finality | Bids received | How it ended\n1 | 1 | 2013-06-24 00:00:00 | Special Committee advanced 50 parties (15 strategic, 35 financial) and began outreach; informational package asked for preliminary indications by 07/23 | 20 admitted: 2 strategic (Party A, CSC/Pamplona); 18 financial (incl. Party B, Party C) | 07/23/2013 | Late bids accepted | Not final | 4: CSC/Pamplona ($18.50); Party B ($17.00-18.00); Party C ($15.00-17.00); Party A (standing 06/21 proposal $17.00-19.00) | Four advanced to management meetings and staged diligence\n1 | 2 | 2013-07-25 00:00:00 | Special Committee advanced four bidders to management presentations and staged data-site access; revised indications to follow | 4: Party A, Party B, Party C, CSC/Pamplona | 09/09/2013 | Late bids accepted | Not final | 4: CSC/Pamplona ($19.50); Party B ($18.50); Party A ($18.00-19.00); Party C ($16.00-17.00) | Four advanced to final indications\n1 | 3 | 2013-09-11 00:00:00 | Special Committee requested final indications by 09/18 and flagged a possible short exclusivity | 4: Party A, Party B, Party C, CSC/Pamplona | 09/18/2013 | Enforced | Announced as final | 3: CSC/Pamplona ($20.75); Party B ($21.50 valued; $19.00 cash plus options); Party A ($18.00-19.00, best and final); Party C none | CSC/Pamplona selected; Party C out\n1 | 4 | 2013-09-19 00:00:00 | Special Committee selected CSC/Pamplona and authorized exclusivity negotiations at $21.25 per share | 1: CSC/Pamplona | none stated | No deadline stated | Inferred final | 1: CSC/Pamplona ($21.25) | signing\n\n===== Questions =====\nQ | Question | Recommended answer | Why, with page | Rows affected | What changes if answered differently | Reviewer note\nQ1 | Round map: I read four rounds, with round 4 opening 09/19/2013 when the Special Committee selected CSC/Pamplona and moved to definitive negotiation; the 09/21 $21.25 last-and-best bid is round 4 (Inferred final). Alternative: three rounds - C8 says a further price-improvement request to the same finalists after a final solicitation, and exclusivity inside an established final stage, are not a new round - so the $21.25 bid sits in round 3 and no round-4 line exists. Which reading? | Keep four rounds: C8 lists moving to definitive negotiation with selected bidders as a new-round trigger, and round 3's competitive solicitation ended when CSC/Pamplona was selected 09/19. | pp. 36-37: after the 09/18 final bids the committee judged CSC/Pamplona most valuable, required it to raise its price above $20.75 and authorized exclusivity at $21.25; exclusivity executed 09/24 (p. 38). | #39, #40; Rounds sheet rounds 3-4 | The three-round reading deletes #39, moves the $21.25 bid into round 3, and changes round 3's 'How it ended' to signing; live-bidder counts are unaffected. | \nQ2 | Deadline outcomes. Round 1 (due 07/23): CSC/Pamplona bid on time, Party B and Party C arrived 07/24 and were reviewed 07/25 - I record 'Late bids accepted'. Round 2 (due 09/09): Party A and Party C submitted 09/10 and were reviewed 09/11 - also 'Late bids accepted'. Round 3 (due 09/18): three responded, Party C none, and the committee acted - 'Enforced'. Alternative: call all three deadlines Enforced? | Keep as recorded. C11 makes 'Late bids accepted' the label when the target considered bids arriving after the due date with no revision; round 3 saw no late bid, so 'Enforced'. | pp. 33-34: 07/24-07/25 bids reviewed at the 07/25 meeting; pp. 35-36: 09/10 bids reviewed 09/11; 09/19 meeting acted on the 09/18 bids (p. 36). | #16, #19, #20, #23, #27, #30, #31, #34; Rounds sheet 'Deadline outcome' | Under 'Enforced', the Rounds sheet would show deadlines as strict and the late rows as unsolicited; live-bidder counts do not change. | \nQ3 | Party A's and Party B's exits: still live after the 09/18 final bids, they were displaced when Mac-Gray executed exclusivity with CSC/Pamplona 09/24; I date both exits 09/24 (inferred, 'Dropped by target'), as C16 directs. Alternative: date them at signing 10/14 as 'Not selected at signing', since the filing never says they were told they were out? | Keep 09/24: the exclusive agreement with a rival displaced them on its execution date; no notice is reported, so mark inferred. | p. 37 (committee selected CSC/Pamplona); p. 38 (exclusivity executed 09/24/2013); the filing reports no notice to Party A or Party B. | #42, #43 | Exits would move to 10/14 with reason 'Not selected at signing', leaving three live bidders from 09/24 to 10/14 instead of one. | \nQ4 | The 16 residual financial NDA signers exit as one inferred cohort 'Did not submit' at the 07/23/2013 due date (20 signers less Party A less 3 submitters = 16). Alternative: some of the residual may have signed after 07/23 and so were never solicited, making the count smaller? | Keep 16 at 07/23: the filing reports one two-month wave of 20 signings; only Party A's is dated later (08/05) and its standing proposal keeps it accounted for. | p. 32: 'Over the next two months a total of 20 potential bidders... entered into confidentiality agreements'; p. 34: Party A's NDA 08/05/2013. | #18, #21 | A smaller eligible base reduces the inferred exits and leaves unsolicited signers unaccounted; totals no longer reconcile to the stated 20. | \n\n===== Deal facts =====\nField | Value\nTarget | Mac-Gray Corporation (laundry facilities management contractor; NYSE-listed)\nAcquirer | CSC ServiceWorks, Inc., with Spin Holdco Inc. (Parent), CSC ServiceWorks Holdings, Inc. and CSC Fenway, Inc.; backed by Pamplona Capital Partners III, L.P.\nAcquirer type | Strategic (sponsor-owned operating company: CSC is an operating laundry-services company owned by the private-equity firm Pamplona)\nAgreed price and consideration | $21.25 per share in cash at closing; no financing contingency; Pamplona committed $594 million\nMerger agreement signed | 2013-10-14 00:00:00\nMerger announced | 2013-10-15 00:00:00\nFiling type and date | DEFM14A definitive proxy statement dated 12/04/2013\nBackground pages | 27-41\nInitiation | Mixed - Mac-Gray/BofA approached Party A on 04/08/2013, but Party A's unsolicited 06/21/2013 proposal and Moab's proxy activism also shaped the launch\nNumber of processes | 1\nAuction screen | Met - 20 independent prospective acquirers executed confidentiality agreements (2 strategic, 18 financial)\nWhole-company bids | Yes - all bids were for the whole company\nCurrency and units of bid prices | US dollars per share (Party B's 09/18/2013 bid valued at $21.50 per share included options it valued at $2.50)\nTarget financial advisers | BofA Merrill Lynch (primary; engaged 10/23/2012 for the 2011-2012 Discussions, terminated 05/15/2013, re-engaged 05/31/2013)\nTarget legal advisers | Goodwin Procter LLP\nAccount | Mac-Gray, a laundry facilities management contractor, reviewed strategic alternatives with BofA Merrill Lynch, and on April 8, 2013 BofA approached Party A about a possible combination. After Party A's unsolicited June 21 proposal of $17.00-$19.00 per share, the Special Committee decided on June 24 to explore a sale and BofA approached 50 parties (15 strategic, 35 financial); 20 signed confidentiality agreements. Four bidders emerged - CSC/Pamplona, Party A, Party B and Party C - whose July preliminary indications ranged from $15.00-$17.00 to $18.50. After management presentations and staged diligence, September revised indications were $19.50 (CSC/Pamplona), $18.50 (Party B), $18.00-$19.00 (Party A) and $16.00-$17.00 (Party C). A final round on September 18 brought $20.75 from CSC/Pamplona, a $21.50-valued cash-plus-options package from Party B and a best-and-final $18.00-$19.00 from Party A; Party C did not submit. The Special Committee chose CSC/Pamplona, secured a raise to $21.25, and signed on October 14, announcing October 15.\n\nnon-date values in date cols: []\nnumber formats: MM/DD/YYYY 0.00\ndeal facts date cells: 2013-10-14 00:00:00 MM/DD/YYYY\nheader bold check: True True\nwrap check: True\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"===== Rounds =====\nProcess | Round | Opened | How opened | Who was in | Due dates | Deadline outcome | Finality | Bids received | How it ended\n1 | 1 | 2013-06-24 00:00:00 | Special Committee advanced 50 parties (15 strategic, 35 financial) and began outr