{"type":"text","timestamp":1789825543738,"sessionID":"ses_f461fa331ffe0Vx8rAYcMiXg6i","part":{"id":"prt_0b9ea552c00178IKg6oJ2yKk0c_text-0","sessionID":"ses_f461fa331ffe0Vx8rAYcMiXg6i","messageID":"msg_0b9ea552c00178IKg6oJ2yKk0c","type":"text","text":"The workbook is finished and validated. Here's the delivery summary.\n\n## Deliverable\n\n**`/home/uctpiaj/work/extraction/mac-gray.xlsx`** — four sheets in order: **Deal ledger** (row 1 headers frozen, filters on, wrap text, no merges), **Rounds**, **Questions**, **Deal facts**. Dates are real Excel dates (MM/DD/YYYY); quotes are exact, each cited to the filing's printed page.\n\n## The sale, in short\n\nMac-Gray's board had BofA Merrill Lynch sound out Party A on April 8, 2013. Party A's unsolicited June 21 bid ($17.00–$19.00) helped push the Special Committee, on June 24, to explore a sale: BofA approached 50 parties (15 strategic, 35 financial), 20 signed NDAs, and four bidders emerged — CSC/Pamplona, Party A, Party B and Party C. Four rounds followed: preliminary indications (due 7/23), staged diligence and revised indications (due 9/9), a final round (due 9/18, Announced as final), and, after the committee chose CSC/Pamplona on 9/19, a definitive-negotiation round. CSC/Pamplona raised to $21.25, exclusivity ran from 9/24 (extended to 10/15), and the merger agreement was signed 10/14 and announced 10/15. Party C exited by non-submission; Party A and Party B were dropped when exclusivity was executed; the 16 anonymous financial NDA signers are closed as one inferred \"Did not submit\" cohort.\n\n## Open questions (all on the Questions sheet, with recommendations)\n\n- **Q1 – round map.** I read four rounds, with round 4 opening 9/19 when the target moved to definitive negotiation; the alternative is three (the $21.25 raise and exclusivity being, respectively, a price-improvement request and \"exclusivity inside an established final stage\"). **Recommend: keep four rounds** — C8 lists the move to definitive negotiation with a selected bidder as a new-round trigger, and round 3's competitive stage had ended.\n- **Q2 – deadline outcomes.** Rounds 1 and 2 are recorded \"Late bids accepted\" (7/24 and 9/10 submissions were considered without revision); round 3 \"Enforced\". **Recommend: keep as recorded**, per C11's definition; alternative \"Enforced\" would misread accepted late bids.\n- **Q3 – Party A/Party B exits.** Both dated 9/24 (exclusivity execution), inferred \"Dropped by target\". **Recommend: keep 9/24**; the alternative is \"Not selected at signing\" on 10/14, which would leave three live bidders through early October.\n- **Q4 – residual NDA cohort.** 20 signers − Party A − 3 submitters = 16 exits at the 7/23 due date. **Recommend: keep 16 at 7/23**; only Party A's signing (8/5) is dated after the solicitation and its standing proposal keeps it accounted for.\n\n## What I could not do\n\n- No whole 10/16/2013 \"process terminated\" or go-shop activity exists to record — the filing shows none (no go-shop, no post-signing competing proposals), so no such rows.\n- Party A's outside counsel is unnamed in the filing, so no adviser row; the three buyer-side banks (Morgan Stanley, Deutsche Bank, Evercore) are named only in the merger agreement's fee representation, so they carry \"first seen\" dates of 10/14 and no engagement details.\n- The 2011–2012 Discussions (a possible Mac-Gray purchase of CSC) are recorded only as adviser history, not as a prior sale process; per the instruction it is not Mac-Gray's sale process, so the process count remains 1.","time":{"start":1789825537336,"end":1789825543738}}}
